INT. AUCTION HOUSE - BACK ROOM - DAY

A narrow room behind the sale floor. Velvet-draped easels. A single tall window. Through the wall, the muffled voice of an auctioneer and the occasional rap of a gavel.

CORNELIUS WREN (60s, tweed, silk pocket square) sits at a polished table with a cup of tea, serene. Across from him, PHILIPPA VANTONGEREN (40s, tailored suit, folder in her lap) sits very upright, a pen between her fingers.

CORNELIUS
You'll find the provenance is impeccable.

PHILIPPA
I'm sure.

CORNELIUS
Painted in 1884. Single owner since 1921. Never exhibited.

PHILIPPA
Never exhibited.

CORNELIUS
Never.

He sips. She taps the pen against the folder. Her eyes stay on the cup.

CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
My dear, you look unwell.

PHILIPPA
I'm well.

CORNELIUS
You've hardly touched your biscuit.

PHILIPPA
I don't care for biscuits.

CORNELIUS
No one cares for them. They are an excuse for tea.

She smiles, briefly. The gavel falls in the next room.

CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
So. Shall we sign?

PHILIPPA
Of course.

She opens the folder and slides a document across the table. He reads it, uncaps his fountain pen, and begins to sign.

The door opens. A tall man in a navy suit enters without knocking: DETECTIVE ANSELM OBI (50s, badge on his belt, hands in his pockets). He closes the door behind him.

ANSELM
Mr. Wren.

CORNELIUS
This is a private meeting.

ANSELM
I know. I've been listening to it for twenty minutes.

Philippa sets down her pen. She lifts the collar of her blouse and reveals a small wire.

CORNELIUS
You.

PHILIPPA ^
Me.

Cornelius stands so fast his chair goes over. He lunges for the document; she snatches it. He reaches for the window latch; Anselm catches his arm and twists it behind his back, and they stagger together against the easel, which topples, and a velvet cloth slides off a tarnished gold frame to reveal a painting of a small girl in a blue dress holding an apple.

Silence. The three of them stare.

ANSELM
That's not a Corot.

CORNELIUS
(strangled)
It's a very good copy.

PHILIPPA
It's a very good forgery.

In the sale room, the gavel falls again.
